Redis can help, but first find out **where** the 2 seconds is spent. Caching a slow page without knowing the cause often hides a problem that is cheaper to fix, and it adds invalidation work you'll have to maintain.

## 1. Measure first

- **Browser DevTools → Network tab:** Check whether the time is in the server response (TTFB) or in loading images, JS and CSS. If TTFB is low and the page is still slow, Redis won't help.
- **Server-side timing:** Log how long the database queries take compared with template rendering.
- **Query log / `EXPLAIN`:** Look for slow queries or many repeated ones.

## 2. Common causes Redis would only mask

| Cause | Typical fix |
|---|---|
| N+1 queries (one query per product for images, prices, stock) | Eager-load or join them |
| Missing index on category, status or sort columns | Add the index |
| Loading the whole catalogue with no pagination | Paginate or limit |
| Large unoptimised images | Resize, WebP/AVIF, lazy-load, CDN |
| Expensive per-request work (currency conversion, recalculating discounts) | Precompute it |

Any of these can turn 2s into about 200ms with no cache at all.

## 3. If Redis is still the right choice

Things to get right:

- **Cache key:** Include everything that changes the result, such as category, page, sort, filters and currency/locale.
- **Invalidation:** Clear or refresh the relevant keys when a product, price or stock level changes. Otherwise customers will see stale prices or buy out-of-stock items.
- **TTL as a safety net:** Even with invalidation, set an expiry (for example 5–15 minutes).
- **Don't cache per-user data:** Customer-specific prices, carts or "recently viewed" should be layered on top of the cached catalogue.
- **Stampede protection:** When a popular key expires, avoid many requests rebuilding it at the same moment. A short lock or early refresh handles this.
- **Fail open:** If Redis is down, fall back to the database instead of erroring.

A basic cache-aside pattern in Node:

```javascript
// Build a cache key from every option that changes the catalogue result,
// so different categories, pages and sort orders never share a cache entry
function buildCatalogueCacheKey(categoryId, pageNumber, sortOrder) {
    return `catalogue:${categoryId}:${pageNumber}:${sortOrder}`;
}

// Return the catalogue from Redis if we have it, otherwise load it from the database
// and store it in Redis so the next request is fast
const getCatalogue = async (redisClient, database, categoryId, pageNumber, sortOrder) => {
    const cacheKey = buildCatalogueCacheKey(categoryId, pageNumber, sortOrder);
    const cacheLifetimeInSeconds = 600;

    // Try the cache first. If Redis is unavailable we log it and carry on,
    // because a slow page is better than a broken one
    try {
        const cachedCatalogue = await redisClient.get(cacheKey);

        if (cachedCatalogue !== null) {
            return JSON.parse(cachedCatalogue);
        }
    } catch (error) {
        console.error("Redis read failed, falling back to database", error);
    }

    // Cache miss, so load the products from the database
    const catalogue = await database.getProducts(categoryId, pageNumber, sortOrder);

    // Save the result with an expiry so stale data can never live forever
    try {
        await redisClient.set(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(catalogue), { EX: cacheLifetimeInSeconds });
    } catch (error) {
        console.error("Redis write failed, result not cached", error);
    }

    return catalogue;
};
```

## Recommendation

Spend 30 minutes profiling before you build the cache. If the cause is a missing index or N+1 queries, fix that. You'll probably still benefit from Redis later, but it will then be speeding up a healthy page instead of covering for a broken one.

