## What to do, roughly in order of impact

**1. Resize to display size.** This is usually the biggest win. If thumbnails display at 300px wide but the files are 2000px+ camera originals, each one is roughly 20–40x larger than it needs to be.

**2. Convert to WebP or AVIF.** These formats are typically 25–50% smaller than JPEG at the same visual quality, and all current browsers support them.

**3. Lazy-load images below the fold.** The browser then only downloads what's on screen at first:

```html
<img src="/images/products/kettle-400.webp" alt="Stainless steel kettle"
     width="400" height="400" loading="lazy">
```

Keep `width` and `height` so the layout doesn't jump as images arrive. Leave the first row or two **without** `loading="lazy"`, since those should load immediately.

**4. Serve responsive sizes** so phones don't download desktop-sized images:

```html
<img src="/images/products/kettle-400.webp"
     srcset="/images/products/kettle-400.webp 400w, /images/products/kettle-800.webp 800w"
     sizes="(max-width: 600px) 50vw, 300px"
     alt="Stainless steel kettle" width="400" height="400" loading="lazy">
```

**5. Set long cache headers on images** (e.g. `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable` with versioned filenames). This only helps repeat visits; first loads still need steps 1–4.

## Batch-converting the existing images

If you're on Node, [sharp](https://sharp.pixelplumbing.com/) can resize and convert the whole folder in one pass:

```javascript
const fs = require("fs/promises");
const path = require("path");
const sharp = require("sharp");

// Widths we generate for each product image, matching the sizes used in the srcset attribute
const outputWidths = [400, 800];

// Resize one original image to each target width and save it as WebP
// Originals are left untouched so we can regenerate with different settings later
const convertImage = async (sourcePath, outputFolder) => {
    const baseName = path.parse(sourcePath).name;

    for (const width of outputWidths) {
        const outputPath = path.join(outputFolder, `${baseName}-${width}.webp`);

        // withoutEnlargement stops small originals from being upscaled and getting blurry
        await sharp(sourcePath)
            .resize({ width: width, withoutEnlargement: true })
            .webp({ quality: 80 })
            .toFile(outputPath);
    }
};

// Convert every JPEG and PNG in the source folder and write the results to the output folder
async function convertAllImages(sourceFolder, outputFolder) {
    await fs.mkdir(outputFolder, { recursive: true });
    const fileNames = await fs.readdir(sourceFolder);

    for (const fileName of fileNames) {
        // Skip anything that isn't a photo format we want to convert, such as .DS_Store
        if (!/\.(jpe?g|png)$/i.test(fileName)) {
            continue;
        }

        await convertImage(path.join(sourceFolder, fileName), outputFolder);
        console.log(`Converted ${fileName}`);
    }
}

convertAllImages("./images/originals", "./images/products");
```

Done properly, 6 MB of images typically drops to a few hundred KB on first load. That should bring the page well under a second.

